Architecture Flow: Data Entry to Dashboard Display
This document outlines the intended lifecycle of a transaction within the application, utilizing Zustand for state management and Supabase for backend processing.

1. Application Initialization (The Ignition)
The flow begins the moment the user opens the application, before the dashboard is even rendered.

Mounting: App.jsx is rendered.

Auth Handshake: App.jsx calls initAuth() from useKingdomStore.

Session Validation: Zustand asks Supabase for the current active session.

Data Hydration: Once the user.id is confirmed, initAuth automatically triggers a batch of fetch requests:

fetchTransactions() (Gets raw ledger data)

fetchDashboardMetrics() (Gets top-level KPIs via RPC)

fetchAnalytics() (Gets pre-calculated chart data from Views)

fetchKingdomData() (Gets Gamification stats)

UI Unblocking: Only after the user is validated does App.jsx allow the Dashboard component to render.

2. Data Entry & Optimistic UI (The Input)
When the user submits a new transaction via the UI (e.g., clicking "Add Transaction").

Payload Creation: The UI sends the raw data (amount, type, category, etc.) to the addTransaction function in useKingdomStore.

Optimistic Update: Zustand immediately creates a temporary ID for the transaction and injects it into the local state (transactions array) so the UI feels instant.

Database Insertion: Zustand sends a POST request to the Supabase transactions table.

Critical Rule: The payload attaches the user.id as the profile_id.

Data Sync: Upon successful database insertion, addTransaction forces the application to fetch the latest server-side aggregations by triggering:

fetchDashboardMetrics()

fetchAnalytics()

3. Server-Side Aggregation (The Engine)
Once the raw transaction hits the database, the frontend does not do the math. The database handles the heavy lifting.

Row Level Security (RLS): Supabase intercepts the query. It checks if the profile_id on the transaction matches the authenticated user requesting it. If it doesn't, Supabase hides the row entirely.

RPC Calculation: The get_dashboard_metrics Remote Procedure Call calculates total_assets, total_liabilities, and net_worth directly on the server for that specific user.

SQL Views: The database automatically updates its materialized views or standard views (e.g., vw_monthly_analytics, vw_category_balances, vw_account_balances) based on the new raw data.

4. Dashboard Rendering (The Output)
How the visual components consume the prepared data.

Dashboard Mounts: Dashboard.jsx renders the layout shell and calls hydrateLayouts() to position the widgets on the grid.

Widget Subscription: Individual widgets inside DashboardCanvas (e.g., a Bar Chart or a KPI Card) subscribe to specific slices of the Zustand store.

A KPI widget reads from state.dashboardMetrics.total_assets.

A Chart widget reads from state.analytics.monthly.

Reactive UI: Because the components are hooked into Zustand, any time fetchAnalytics() finishes pulling new data from Supabase, the store updates, and the widgets instantly re-render with the new numbers.